;; Database-side guarantee that account emails are unique regardless of case.
;;
;; The service layer already writes a lowercased email_normalized, and 001 puts
;; a UNIQUE on that column. This adds the matching guard on the raw value so
;; that any future write which bypasses the normaliser cannot create a second
;; account differing only by case.
;;
;; This matters because Postgres TEXT UNIQUE is case sensitive: without it,
;; Test@example.com and test@example.com are two accounts, and password reset
;; then mails whichever row happens to match.
[

 {:id "010-add-case-insensitive-email-uniqueness"
  :up ["CREATE UNIQUE INDEX accounts_email_normalized_key ON accounts (lower(email_normalized));"]
  :down ["DROP INDEX accounts_email_normalized_key;"]}

 ]
